---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Technical Program Management for an AI System · San Lee"
  description="I frame an AI system as a technical program. The frame is its roadmap, its dependencies, its success measures, its delivery risks, and its evidence-based scope decisions."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/product-and-program.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-product-and-program.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* JTBD pipeline strip (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: open four-stage
       flow for this product framing, not a shared diagram type.

       The plate geometry moved to the gallery ladder (ADR-014). The wrapper
       is .fig.fig--plate, and style.css caps the artwork at --fig-plate. This
       rule therefore sets no width, no max-width, and no margin. The ladder
       is the one owner of all three.

       The 1:1 unit discipline goes with them, so this plate owes a
       measurement. I measured it on 2026-08-31. The plate renders at 272px in
       a 320px viewport, which is scale 1.046. Declared units meet the 9px
       microtext floor here. The scale does not meet it for them. */
    .jtbd-strip .jtbd-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .jtbd-strip .jtbd-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). */
    .jtbd-strip .jtbd-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .jtbd-strip .jtbd-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Stacked feed marks into Firehose — schematic only, not a UI chrome. */
    .jtbd-strip .jtbd-glyph {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.25;
      stroke-linecap: round;
    }
    .jtbd-strip marker path { fill: var(--muted); }

    /* Now / Next / Later plate. Page-scoped: sequence, not the item list
       (the three columns below carry those). Solid / hairline / dashed
       dual-encode horizon. No oxide: this is a roadmap, not a reversal.
       Geometry: the gallery ladder owns it, as on .jtbd-strip above. */
    .nnl-plate .nn-now {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .nnl-plate .nn-next {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .nnl-plate .nn-later {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .nnl-plate .nn-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). */
    .nnl-plate .nn-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }

    /* Capacity-gate plate (portfolio ADR-004, ADR-012). Page-scoped: the three
       moves that made the review a measurement rather than an opinion. Same
       geometry as the homepage .method-plate on purpose — this gate is that
       method turned on the build capacity itself. Open pipeline, no oxide: the
       gate returned a clear answer, and a step down on evidence is not a
       reversal. It carries no number, so it cannot go stale. ADR-010 N/A.
       Geometry: the gallery ladder owns it, as on .jtbd-strip above. */
    .gp-plate .gp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .gp-plate .gp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). */
    .gp-plate .gp-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .gp-plate .gp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .gp-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }

    /* The two-slot caption grammar left this page (ADR-014, OC-F closed).

       This page carried a COPY of the origin block in
       src/pages/projects/context-architecture.astro at commit 9d81caa (pull
       request #247). The copy existed because a concurrent session owned
       public/assets/style.css and two writers on one stylesheet cannot merge.
       That session merged. The MONOGRAPH integration then gave .fig, .fig
       figcaption, .fig-what, and .fig-limit one home in style.css. This lane
       deletes the copy rather than keep it in step by hand.

       Three single-slot captions sit on this page. They caption bare plates
       inside a div.fig wrapper and not inside a figure element. They hold no
       .fig-limit, and no gate reads them. Each one carries the class fig-what,
       which puts it in the consolidated note voice — and style.css now gives
       it the consolidated BOX as well, through `.fig--plate > p`. The
       page-scoped rule that set that box is deleted with the rest: the class
       names stay on the markup as hooks, and nothing here declares. */

    /* pp1: the axis board. A STRIP, so it re-lays text and draws nothing.
       Two lists sit under this section today, and the reader has to join a
       result to its ruling by hand. The board puts them in one grammar: the
       claim, then the evidence. That grammar is the homepage ledger's.

       Two columns above 620px and one column below it, so every row reads at
       320px. min-width: 0 on every cell, because a grid item defaults to its
       content's minimum width and a long word then widens the page. */
    .pp1-rows {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .pp1-row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 4px 20px;
      padding: 12px 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .pp1-claim {
      margin: 0;
      min-width: 0;
      color: var(--text);
      font-weight: 600;
      font-size: 0.96rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }
    .pp1-detail {
      margin: 0;
      min-width: 0;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.58;
    }
    .pp1-group {
      margin: 26px 0 0;
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    @media (min-width: 620px) {
      .pp1-row { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr); }
    }

    /* pp2: three independent measurements, one verdict. The claim recurs on
       four pages and is drawn on none, so it is drawn once here. Hand-drawn,
       so it holds no digit: the figures stay in the bullets above it. The
       gallery ladder owns the geometry (ADR-014), as on .jtbd-strip above.
       Its own marker id and its own marker, because a marker does not inherit
       the stroke of the path that references it. */
    .pp2-plate .pp2-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .pp2-plate .pp2-verdict {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .pp2-plate .pp2-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }
    /* 9px, not 8px: the microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). */
    .pp2-plate .pp2-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .pp2-plate .pp2-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .pp2-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }

    /* The register's pointer row holds the only link inside a table cell on
       this site, so no shared rule ever gave it a colour and it painted the
       browser default. That default measured 1.97:1 in the dark theme and the
       contrast gate failed the build. It points at the accent token now, which
       is the colour every other link on this page already uses. The underline
       stays, so the link is not marked by colour alone. */
    .article-body table a { color: var(--accent); }

    /* pp3: the one risk in the register that happened. A STRIP again: the row
       moves, and no word of it changes its meaning. Inside a three-column
       table that row read at the same weight as five risks that never fired,
       and its 50-word cell is unreadable at 320px. No oxide here. A risk that
       fired is not a decision that I took back, and --accent-warm means only
       the second thing. */
    .pp3-rows {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .pp3-row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2px 20px;
      padding: 10px 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .pp3-rows > .pp3-row:first-child { border-top: 0; }
    .pp3-label {
      margin: 0;
      min-width: 0;
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    .pp3-value {
      margin: 0;
      min-width: 0;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.58;
    }
    .pp3-value strong {
      color: var(--text);
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    @media (min-width: 620px) {
      .pp3-row { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 8fr); }
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Product &amp; Program</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">I frame the same system as a product, and I run it as a program. Solo build. The program layer is simulated.</p>

    <div class="decision" aria-label="Case study summary">
      <p class="tag">Case study summary</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Problem:</strong> Reduce the time a defense-news analyst spends finding and sorting developments before analysis can begin.</li>
        <li><strong>Ownership:</strong> I acted as product owner and TPM for a solo build, turning delivery into a roadmap, dependency map, success measures, and risk register.</li>
        <li><strong>Constraints:</strong> There was no team or external design partner, so cross-team coordination and user-outcome claims could not be represented as real.</li>
        <li><strong>Actions:</strong> I defined the job to be done, sequenced Now / Next / Later work, tied program artifacts to shipped work, and used eval results to manage quality, cost, and scope risk.</li>
        <li><strong>Result and evidence:</strong> The asynchronous enrichment loop, tool-layer contract, and classifier CI gate shipped. Classification is measured; analyst outcome metrics are explicitly defined but not yet validated in real use.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="lead">
      This is a solo build. There is no team to coordinate, so the program
      layer is simulated. The workstreams, the dependency map, and the risk
      register are real work. I did that work as if a team existed.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem (product framing)</h2>
    <p>
      Defense developments come from many sources. They arrive faster than one
      person can triage. An analyst spends a disproportionate share of the time
      to find them and to sort them. Real analysis begins after that.
    </p>

    <!-- Open pipeline, and not a second copy of the JTBD when/so card:
         Firehose → Triage → Queryable KB → Brief. Three L→R muted edges only.
         No return edge, and no oxide, because this is not a failure claim and
         not a reversal claim. ADR-010 does not apply. There is no interactive
         node here.

         Plate 01 of six (ADR-014). A bare plate takes a div.fig wrapper and
         never a figure element. A figure carries the two-slot caption rule,
         and this plate holds one caption slot. data-place="spine" states the
         width policy for the kind: a plate sits on the content track. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
    <svg class="jtbd-strip" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="Triage sorts the defense-news firehose by relevance and category into a queryable knowledge base. The analyst briefs from a prepared store, and not from a raw feed. Stages in order: Firehose, Triage, Queryable KB, Brief.">
      <title>Triage sorts the defense-news firehose by relevance and category into a queryable knowledge base. The analyst briefs from a prepared store, and not from a raw feed. Stages in order: Firehose, Triage, Queryable KB, Brief.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="jtbd-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Four stage boxes. rx matches --radius-sm (2px). At max-width 260 one
           viewBox unit is one CSS pixel. Queryable KB runs on two lines, so the
           short labels fit with no horizontal overflow at 320. -->
      <rect class="jtbd-box" x="2" y="6" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <!-- Stacked feed marks. They draw the raw multi-source stream into
           Firehose. -->
      <g class="jtbd-glyph" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M 12 14 H 28" />
        <path d="M 12 18 H 32" />
        <path d="M 12 22 H 26" />
      </g>
      <text class="jtbd-title" x="30" y="36" text-anchor="middle">Firehose</text>

      <rect class="jtbd-box" x="68" y="6" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="jtbd-title" x="96" y="30" text-anchor="middle">Triage</text>

      <rect class="jtbd-box" x="134" y="6" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="jtbd-title" x="162" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Queryable</text>
      <text class="jtbd-title" x="162" y="36" text-anchor="middle">KB</text>

      <rect class="jtbd-box" x="200" y="6" width="56" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="jtbd-title" x="228" y="30" text-anchor="middle">Brief</text>

      <!-- Three L→R muted arrows only. The pipeline is open, and it has no
           return edge. -->
      <path class="jtbd-edge" d="M 58 26 L 66 26" marker-end="url(#jtbd-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="jtbd-edge" d="M 124 26 L 132 26" marker-end="url(#jtbd-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="jtbd-edge" d="M 190 26 L 198 26" marker-end="url(#jtbd-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

      <!-- Geist Mono microcopy under each stage. Each label stays short, so
           two adjacent labels do not collide at 260 viewBox width. The centres
           sit about 66 units apart. -->
      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="30" y="60" text-anchor="middle">raw feed</text>
      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="96" y="60" text-anchor="middle">rel+category</text>
      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="162" y="60" text-anchor="middle">ask store</text>
      <text class="jtbd-micro" x="228" y="60" text-anchor="middle">analyze</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="fig-what jtbd-strip-caption">
      Firehose in; brief out. Triage and a queryable knowledge base sit in the
      middle, so the analyst does not spend analysis time on the sort.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Primary user:</strong> a defense-news analyst. The analyst must
      stay current across programs, contracts, geopolitics, and technology, and
      must brief others on what matters. Defense is a deliberately chosen
      vertical, and not a claim of domain expertise.
      <strong>Design partner:</strong> me. I use the system myself, as the first
      user and the early validation loop.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Job to be done</p>
      <p>When a stream of defense news comes in, help me quickly know <strong>what
        matters</strong> and <strong>where it fits</strong>, so I spend my time
        analyzing instead of sorting.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Success metrics</h2>
    <p>Two kinds. They answer different questions.</p>

    <h3>User-outcome metrics (does it help the analyst?)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Auto-triage rate:</strong> share of incoming items correctly categorized automatically, so the analyst never hand-sorts them.</li>
      <li><strong>Coverage (recall):</strong> the share of genuinely relevant developments the system finds rather than misses.</li>
      <li><strong>Answer usefulness:</strong> for a query, the agent returns a correct, cited answer, fast.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ex-caption">I defined these outcome metrics. I did not measure them yet. An honest measurement needs the analyst in the loop over real use, and this is a solo build. What I measure today is classification quality, below.</p>

    <h3>AI-quality metrics (is there a measurement, or only a demo?)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Classification quality:</strong> per-field precision, recall, F1. On real, human-labeled text the current v3 classifier scores category <span data-metric="category_accuracy">94.4%</span> (macro-F1 <span data-metric="category_macro_f1">0.930</span>). It scores operational domain <span data-metric="domain_accuracy">98.1%</span> (<span data-metric="domain_macro_f1">0.982</span>), and region <span data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span> (<span data-metric="region_macro_f1">0.975</span>). I corroborated the figures at n=300. The confidence intervals are half as wide as the 54-snippet set allows.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost per unit of quality:</strong> I built the premium-model escalation and I measured it. I did not assume it. It moved zero rows at about 1.97&times; the cost, so the shipped path stays single-model. It is the second of three independent measurements with the same verdict. The other two are the retired retrieval layer and the <a href="faithfulness-judge.html">judge-reliability study</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Retrieval quality (RAG):</strong> recall@k, answer groundedness, citation correctness.</li>
      <li><strong>Regression gate:</strong> an eval pass-rate threshold wired into CI, so a change that drops below it does not merge.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- pp2-converge (ADR-013). Three independent measurements, one verdict.
         The claim sits in the "Cost per unit of quality" bullet above, it
         recurs on four pages, and no page draws it. It is drawn once here, and
         the other pages link to this page rather than redraw it. The plate is
         hand-drawn, so it holds no digit: every figure stays in the bullets.
         Three muted edges converge on one verdict box. No oxide, because a
         measurement that returns a "no" is not a decision taken back.
         ADR-010 does not apply. There is no interactive node here. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate" data-place="spine">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 02</span>
      <svg class="pp2-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 96" role="img"
           aria-label="Three boxes point down into one verdict box. The three are the retired retrieval layer, the premium-model escalation, and the judge-reliability study. Each one is marked measured. The verdict box reads: the same verdict.">
        <title>Three independent measurements, and one verdict.</title>
        <defs>
          <marker id="pp2-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <!-- Three measurement boxes, each named by the words the bullets use.
             The microcopy is the same word under all three, because the shared
             property is the argument: each one was measured. -->
        <rect class="pp2-box" x="2" y="4" width="78" height="34" rx="2" />
        <text class="pp2-title" x="41" y="20" text-anchor="middle">RETRIEVAL</text>
        <text class="pp2-micro" x="41" y="31" text-anchor="middle">measured</text>

        <rect class="pp2-box" x="91" y="4" width="78" height="34" rx="2" />
        <text class="pp2-title" x="130" y="20" text-anchor="middle">ESCALATION</text>
        <text class="pp2-micro" x="130" y="31" text-anchor="middle">measured</text>

        <rect class="pp2-box" x="180" y="4" width="78" height="34" rx="2" />
        <text class="pp2-title" x="219" y="20" text-anchor="middle">JUDGE STUDY</text>
        <text class="pp2-micro" x="219" y="31" text-anchor="middle">measured</text>

        <!-- Three edges land on three separate points of the verdict box, so
             three arrow heads never stack on one point. -->
        <path class="pp2-edge" d="M 41 38 L 41 50 L 70 50 L 70 58"
              marker-end="url(#pp2-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <path class="pp2-edge" d="M 130 38 L 130 58"
              marker-end="url(#pp2-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <path class="pp2-edge" d="M 219 38 L 219 50 L 190 50 L 190 58"
              marker-end="url(#pp2-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

        <rect class="pp2-verdict" x="42" y="60" width="176" height="32" rx="2" />
        <text class="pp2-title" x="130" y="76" text-anchor="middle">THE SAME VERDICT</text>
        <text class="pp2-micro" x="130" y="87" text-anchor="middle">three independent measurements</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">Three separate measurements returned the same verdict: the retired retrieval layer, the premium-model escalation, and the judge-reliability study. I built each one and I measured it. I did not assume any of them.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">The plate does not show what each measurement measured, or by how much it moved. The bullets above keep those figures, and the judge-reliability study keeps its own page.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Roadmap (program framing)</h2>
    <p>I sequenced the work Now / Next / Later. I harvested the sequence from delivery.</p>
    <!-- Plate 03 of six (ADR-014). Bare plate, so the wrapper is a div.fig
         and not a figure element. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 03</span>
    <svg class="nnl-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="Program sequence. Now is shipped, Next is queued, Later is deferred. Later includes explicit non-goals.">
      <title>Now shipped, Next queued, Later deferred.</title>
      <rect class="nn-now" x="2" y="6" width="80" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="nn-title" x="42" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Now</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="42" y="38" text-anchor="middle">shipped</text>
      <rect class="nn-next" x="90" y="6" width="80" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="nn-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Next</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">queued</text>
      <rect class="nn-later" x="178" y="6" width="80" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="nn-title" x="218" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Later</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="218" y="38" text-anchor="middle">deferred</text>
      <text class="nn-micro" x="130" y="64" text-anchor="middle">harvested from delivery</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="fig-what nnl-plate-caption">
      Solid is shipped. Hairline is queued. Dashed is deferred, and it includes
      the things I will not build.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="roadmap">
      <div>
        <h3>Now</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Async enrichment loop: create, classify, idempotent writeback</li>
          <li>Integration tests across both seams (pytest, fully offline)</li>
          <li>Agent tool-layer contract (SYS-003), accepted and built</li>
          <li>Evals-as-CI: the classifier&rsquo;s gold evals gate every PR</li>
          <li>Product one-pager and this program view</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Next</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Evals-as-CI for kb-agent: extend the gate to retrieval</li>
          <li>A weekly status cadence from real progress</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Later</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Containerize and deploy the services</li>
          <li>OpenTelemetry observability across services</li>
          <li>Other verticals: an explicit non-goal, articulated not built</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Risk register</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Risk</th><th>Severity</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Enrichment lost if the background task fails or the process restarts (no broker redelivery)</td><td>Low</td><td>Tags are derived and reclassification is idempotent (namespaced-tag replace), so enrichment re-runs safely. Note creation never fails on classifier errors</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Classifier accuracy ceiling (residual label ambiguity, not the model)</td><td>Medium</td><td>Do not escalate the model. I measured it at +0 rows for ~1.97&times; cost. Refine the rubric instead. A confusion breakdown found the misses clustered on one boundary, and two clauses lifted category 90.7% &rarr; 94.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Breadth creep (verticals without depth)</td><td>Medium</td><td>Deep on one vehicle; other verticals are an explicit non-goal</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Planning theater (docs drift from delivery)</td><td>Medium</td><td>Keep the artifacts thin and current, and tie them to real delivery only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Silent contract drift (separate repos)</td><td colspan="2">This is the one risk that happened. The <a href="#risk-drift">callout below</a> carries the severity, what happened, the open status, and the fix</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Simulated program (no real cross-team coordination)</td><td>Low (honesty)</td><td>I state it plainly. The reasoning and the artifacts are real. The org is not</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- pp-s2 (ADR-013). Row 5 of the register leaves the severity cell. It is
