    <h2 id="capacity-gate">I pre-registered six axes for a capacity review, then measured that nothing wrote to them.</h2>
    <p>
      This record applies an evidence gate to a model choice. I applied the same
      gate to the build capacity behind the work. On a fixed date I wrote the
      review down. It named six evidence axes, a close date, and a rule that
      nothing changes until the close. Then I let the axes decide.
    </p>

    <!-- Plate 05 of six (ADR-014). Bare plate, so the wrapper is a div.fig
         and not a figure element. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate" data-place="spine">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 05</span>
    <svg class="gp-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="How I ran the capacity review. I pre-registered the evidence axes before any evidence existed. I froze every change until the close. Then I ruled on the record. The gate returned a step down.">
      <title>Pre-register the axes, freeze until the close, rule on the record.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="gp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <rect class="gp-box" x="2" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="40" y="24" text-anchor="middle">PRE-REGISTER</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="40" y="38" text-anchor="middle">axes first</text>
      <path class="gp-edge" d="M 78 26 L 90 26" marker-end="url(#gp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="gp-box" x="92" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">FREEZE</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">no changes</text>
      <path class="gp-edge" d="M 168 26 L 180 26" marker-end="url(#gp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="gp-box" x="182" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="220" y="24" text-anchor="middle">CLOSE</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="220" y="38" text-anchor="middle">on the record</text>
      <text class="gp-micro" x="130" y="64" text-anchor="middle">the gate said step down</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="fig-what gp-plate-caption">
      Two design choices did all the work. I wrote the axes before the evidence
      existed, and nothing changed until the close.
    </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Those two choices are the whole method. I wrote the axes first, so nothing
      could pick its own success criterion after the fact. I froze every change
      until the close, so nothing could be cut on a bad week or kept on a good
      anecdote. The close came early, on a full month of evidence. One reason
      justifies an early close: the last stretch added no new evidence on any
      axis.
    </p>

    <h3>The results, by axis</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>This section reports by evidence axis. It never reports by lane
      and never by name.</strong> An axis is a question. A lane is a seat, and a
      seat maps to somebody&rsquo;s product. A report by lane turns a
      one-operator measurement into a public claim about a third party. A
      measurement is not an opinion. A table around an opinion does not make it
      a measurement.
    </p>

    <!-- pp-s1 (ADR-013). A STRIP: it re-lays the two ledger lists of this
         section as one board, and it draws nothing. Two lists made the reader
         join a result to its ruling by hand. The board holds six rows because
         the prose holds six items, and every row keeps its own words.

         It deliberately does NOT enumerate the six axes as six rows. Four of
         them carry no named identity anywhere in the record, so a row for each
         axis would need an invented name, or a blank cell that begs to be
         filled. The axis with no result keeps the words the prose uses.

         BOUNDARY, and the writer is the only control here. No gate reads any
         of it. Nothing in this board, its caption, or its aria-label names a
         vendor, a lane, a seat identity, a dollar figure, an account or
         billing detail, a machine identity, a repository of any kind, or
         anything internal to the employer. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="strip" data-place="spine"
            aria-label="The capacity review as one board. Three rows report what the axes produced. Three rows state the rulings that followed.">
      <span class="fig-label">Strip 06</span>
      <ul class="pp1-rows">
        <li class="pp1-row">
          <p class="pp1-claim">One axis produced repeated, measured value.</p>
          <p class="pp1-detail">Independent review loops raised real findings. I confirmed and fixed most of them before merge, and the noise rate was nil. This is the one axis with demonstrated unique value.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pp1-row">
          <p class="pp1-claim">The capacity axis produced the opposite of what I expected.</p>
          <p class="pp1-detail">There were no friction events at all. The record goes the other way: dated directives in my own operating rules tell sessions to spend the headroom rather than to conserve it. Capacity was never the binding constraint.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pp1-row">
          <p class="pp1-claim">The remaining axes produced no dated unique result.</p>
          <p class="pp1-detail">The month gave them the chance to produce one.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="pp1-group">The rulings, as principles</h3>
      <ul class="pp1-rows">
        <li class="pp1-row">
          <p class="pp1-claim">Capacity that was never consumed is unproven, not proven.</p>
          <p class="pp1-detail">So the top tier steps down. The first month on the lower tier is itself the next measurement.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pp1-row">
          <p class="pp1-claim">I declined a parked upgrade.</p>
          <p class="pp1-detail">No recorded event showed that capacity ever blocked anything.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="pp1-row">
          <p class="pp1-claim">A seat is retained for demonstrated unique value.</p>
          <p class="pp1-detail">Attachment to a tier is not evidence.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">The board argues that the record was lopsided. What the axes produced sits beside the rulings that followed, so the reader does not have to join them by hand.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">The board names no seat, no product, and no lane. It reports the axes only, and it does not close the review. The limit on the whole reading sits below it.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    {/* Owner call, still open: does this section name figures, or only the
        mechanism? It ships with the mechanism and no figures. A release is
        three sentences and nothing else. One sentence goes above this block.
        One clause goes in the first ruling. One sentence goes in the closing
        paragraph. These markers are Astro comments, not HTML comments, so they
        do not reach the published page. A note about what a page deliberately
        withholds must not itself be published. */}
    <p class="ex-caption">
      The limit on this reading, at the point of the claim: one month, one
      operator, one body of work, and no controlled comparison. It is a routing
      decision for me. It is not a verdict on anybody&rsquo;s product.
    </p>

    <p class="tradeoff">
      <strong>The result:</strong> I pre-registered the six axes and nothing
      ever wrote to them. There was no friction log, no tally file, and no
      dead-rows list. So I had to reconstruct the evidence at the end out of
      dated records. The gate still returned a clear answer, because the answer
      was lopsided. <strong>A closer result would have been unrecoverable.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The lesson: pre-registration without an instrument is half a measurement.
      I built a gauge for this same capacity, and it does not write this log
      today. That is the open item. I name it here, because that is the only
      honest place to end.
    </p>
